account.body:
The desktop app needs no account for its 30-day trial. Portal accounts are for the online tools, the bookstore and buying activation codes.

## Register and sign in

Click "免费注册" (Sign up free) at the top right of the portal home page, register with an email, then sign in. That unlocks the web writing workbench and the online bookstore.

## Trial and activation

- Every desktop install starts a 30-day full-feature trial — identical to the licensed version;
- After the trial the app turns read-only; your work and data stay untouched;
- Buy a lifetime activation code via "购买永久授权" on the portal (one-time purchase, future updates included);
- Open "Settings → About" in the desktop app and enter the code;
- One code binds one machine; you can migrate it when you switch devices.

settings.body:
Settings are grouped into 17 sections with search support. This page walks through what each section does; appearance and themes are covered in [Themes & Appearance](#/writing/appearance).

## General and appearance

- **General**: light / dark / follow-system, interface language (简体中文 / English), wallpaper, and the creation workspace — pick the folder the Creation page reads; changing it never deletes files in the old folder;
- **Themes**: 10 built-in themes plus custom ones — see [Themes & Appearance](#/writing/appearance);
- **Shortcuts**: every app-level shortcut is remappable.

## Creation and AI

- **Global search**: index statistics and rebuilding;
- **AI services**: chat and image model setup — see [AI Model Setup](#/quickstart/aiSetup);
- **Web tools**: give AI search and page reading via Bocha, Serper, Jina or a custom endpoint;
- **Network proxy**: proxies AI model requests only (http / https / socks5);
- **AI call logs**: per-call records, usage and session aggregates; conversation content is not recorded by default; export to CSV / JSON;
- **MCP services**: the switch, tool permissions and client setup for external AI — see [MCP & External AI](#/publish/mcp);
- **Runtime**: Node/npm detection and the app-managed download for plugin backends — see [Plugin Development Process](#/plugins/dev).

## Sync and publishing

- **Remote sync**: the Git / WebDAV / S3 channels and snapshots — see [Sync Across Devices](#/quickstart/sync);
- **Browser**: start page, search engine and clipping extensions;
- **Notifications**: image generation finished, PDF index ready, sync failures — can be limited to when the window is not focused.

## Misc

- **Privacy & feedback**: usage statistics switch (never includes body text or keys) and the feedback form;
- **Diagnostics log**: startup timing, jank and errors, for troubleshooting;
- **Storage**: cache and agent history cleanup;
- **About**: version and activation status.

sync.body:
Desktop and mobile stay in sync through one remote. Three channels — pick whichever you already have an account for:

- **Git repository**: a private HTTPS repo on GitHub, GitLab, Gitee etc., authenticated with a username + access token;
- **WebDAV**: Nutstore (坚果云), Nextcloud, Synology and similar;
- **S3-compatible storage**: AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO and similar.

## Desktop

1. Open "Settings → Remote sync";
2. Pick a channel and fill in the address, account and token;
3. Save and enable sync. It then runs on a schedule, with the status shown in the top bar.

## Mobile

Open "Settings → Sync & storage", choose the same channel as the desktop and point it at the same remote — both devices now share one dataset.

Mobile auto-sync runs every 5 / 10 / 30 / 60 minutes (default 10), plus on app start and whenever it returns to the foreground; "Sync now" is always available. The cloud icon at the top right shows the sync status.

## When changes conflict

Concurrent edits merge automatically: text gets a three-way merge, overlapping changes keep both sides; unmergeable binary files keep the local version. The pre-merge original is backed up locally, so nothing is lost. Both ends can also keep workspace snapshots on the remote (1–50 recent, your choice) and browse that history directly.

mobileInstall.body:
The mobile app comes in Android and iOS builds and shares the same file formats and sync protocol as the desktop.

## Install

Get BigLazyCat Writer from an app store or the official site. Open it and it is already your creation area — no sign-up needed.

## Know your creation area

- A `.md` file at the root is a single piece; a folder is a collection — what you see in any file manager is what the app shows;
- "思维导图 / 画布 / 演示文稿 / 资料库 / images" are the app's own asset folders;
- `.biglazycat/` is private local data (trash and sync state) — never scanned or synced;
- The search box at the top searches everything; long-press an entry to rename, move or delete it, or multi-select for batch actions — deleted items go to the trash.

## Next steps

- Configure AI: see [AI Model Setup](#/quickstart/aiSetup);
- Connect the desktop: see [Sync Across Devices](#/quickstart/sync).

editor.body:
The body editor is a WYSIWYG Markdown editor: what you type is what it looks like, with a source mode for editing the markup directly.

## How works are organized

Works are organized as folders → files, or exist as single pieces. Drag in the left tree to reorder; the body saves as `.md` files in the creation area, openable in any editor. The writing area is tabbed — several chapters, a mind map and a PDF can be open at once.

## Formatting and inserts

Toolbar buttons or the `/` slash menu insert formats; the basics are in [Markdown Basics](#/syntax/markdownBasics). On top of that:

- Tables: adjustable column widths after insert; right-click to add or remove rows and columns;
- Code blocks: syntax highlighting for common languages; `mermaid` renders as a diagram;
- Formulas: inline and display, with a visual formula editor in the toolbar;
- Images: paste, drag or pick from the library; saved into the image library automatically;
- Image layout rows: two / three / four images side by side, image-left text-right, text-left image-right, or two images with captions;
- Layout blocks: page breaks, collapsible blocks and callouts (info / suggestion / caution / warning);
- TOC: a `[TOC]` marker grows into a jumpable table of contents;
- Embedded mind maps and flow canvases: double-click the embed card to edit;
- An icon and symbol library.

## Outline and annotations

The outline panel shows the whole book's volume/chapter structure, with bulk level adjustments.

Annotations live beside paragraphs, never inside the body: select text to annotate — the highlight is a fixed amber; edit or delete from the annotation list, "show only this one" from the bubble, and click a card to jump back to the source spot. Annotations sync with the chapter to other devices.

## Layout and typing

The settings drawer at the right end of the editor toolbar:

- **Layout**: line height, paragraph spacing, letter spacing (follows the theme by default, overridable) and page padding;
- **Smart typing**: auto spaces between Chinese and Latin, full-width Chinese punctuation, auto-paired brackets and quotes, sentence-case capitalization — each toggleable, and `Ctrl+Z` undoes every auto-fix;
- **Cursor**: smooth caret and movement animation.

The pomodoro timer in the top bar starts a focus round whenever you like, with adjustable phases and rounds; the body autosaves. App-level shortcuts are in [Themes & Appearance](#/writing/appearance); if Vim keys are your habit, see [Vim Editing Mode](#/writing/vim).

## Mobile

The mobile editor shares its core with the desktop:

- Bottom toolbar: outline, AI, insert image, bidirectional link, headings, bold/italic, alignment, lists, quotes and more; undo/redo sit at the top right;
- Double-tap a chapter title to rename it;
- Tapping a paragraph, table or image opens an action panel: convert the paragraph, edit table rows, preview an image with a caption, edit Mermaid source;
- Images come from three sources — camera, album or URL — filed automatically into `images/`;
- Save status is always visible; chapters containing not-yet-supported content degrade to read-only with the original data intact, ready to continue on the desktop.

graph.body:
The relationship graph draws every bidirectional link and tag in the library: articles are dots, tags are diamonds, and lines mean "who references whom". Entry: the "Links" page in the left navigation.

## Exploring the graph

- Articles sit in a circular layout; hovering or selecting shows names and highlights related edges. Display settings add always-on labels and direction arrows, and tune spacing and node size;
- Node size reflects how many neighbors an article has;
- Drag nodes to rearrange, drag the canvas to pan, scroll to zoom; "Fit canvas" resets the view, "Re-layout" restores the automatic arrangement;
- The search box filters by name or path; typing `#tagname` (aliases work) shows the tag and its articles.

## One article's relations

- Click a node: the side panel lists inbound links, outbound links and every quoting snippet; click a snippet to open the source article at that exact link;
- Double-click a node or click "Open article" to start writing;
- "Explore around this": see one to three levels of its network; adjust the depth from the detail panel or return to the whole graph.

## Fixing problem links

- **Unresolved links**: targets that don't exist yet or are ambiguous. Create missing targets directly and existing references connect themselves; fix ambiguous ones from the quoting snippet back at the source;
- **Orphan articles**: content with no links at all. Open it and add `[[` links in the body.

## Tags in the graph

Tags appear as diamond nodes wired to the articles using them; the "show tags" switch hides that layer. Tag editing and bulk operations live on the "Tags" page — see [Tags](#/writing/tags).

The graph covers article links and article tags only; tags attached solely to images or prompts don't enter it. The index updates itself as workspace files change, with a manual refresh available.

prompts.body:
The prompt library stores your frequently used AI instructions: write once, reuse everywhere.

## Create

Open "Settings → Prompts" and create one: name, body and description, with an optional category and a linked image. The more specific the prompt, the steadier the AI performs.

## Manage

- Mark frequently used ones as favorites to pin them;
- Search by name, description, tag and content;
- Sort by last updated, most used, or name;
- One click copies to the clipboard.

## Hooking into AI features

Selection AI modes can bind prompt templates (see [Selection AI](#/ai/aiSelection)); templates accept variable placeholders that are filled with context at call time.

imageGen.body:
The AI image workbench generates images from prompts; finished images land in the image library automatically.

## Generate an image

1. Open the "Images" page in the left navigation and enter the AI image workbench;
2. Pick a prompt template and aspect ratio, write the prompt;
3. Submit — the job queues up while you keep working;
4. Finished images file themselves into the library.

You can also upload an image as the base and have the AI generate on top of it or modify it.

## Managing the library

The "Images" page manages every image: grid and list views, favorites, immersive preview; filter by source (upload / AI generated / web saved), folder, tag and sort order; multi-select for batch delete or download.

Writing illustrations and WeChat covers both come from here — see [WeChat Setup & Publishing](#/publish/wechat).

mindmap.body:
Mind maps organize ideas into trees. A map is a real `.xmind` file stored in the creation area, editable on desktop and mobile alike.

## Desktop

- Create a map in the creation area, or grow a written outline into one with a click; import `.xmind` and Markdown outlines;
- 14 structure presets (logic diagram, mind map, org chart, timeline, fishbone and more) and 14 style presets, with rainbow branches;
- Nodes take icons (emoji / priority / progress / flags), formulas, notes, tags and links; image nodes can be inserted; connect nodes with relation lines and summaries;
- Format painter copies node styles; the style panel tunes shape, fill, border and connector style;
- The outline panel stays in two-way sync with the map: search, expand / collapse by level, follow selection;
- Export to PNG / PDF / XMind / Markdown;
- AI mind-map assistant: conversational creation and editing — select a node first, then describe the change, for more precision; results apply after confirmation;
- Maps can be embedded in the body text; double-click to edit.

## Mobile

- Tap a node, tap again to edit its text; add child or sibling nodes, delete branches;
- Collapse and expand branches, attach notes, undo and redo;
- Three layouts (rightward / both sides / downward), five theme colors, three font sizes;
- Pinch to zoom, one finger to pan.

Advanced content from the desktop — images inside nodes, relation lines and the like — stays intact on mobile as read-only.

canvas.body:
The structured canvas spreads out architecture diagrams, flowcharts and plot lines. A canvas is a `.canvas` file with version management.

## Desktop

- A component library covers common shapes; connectors run straight, orthogonal or Bézier, with arrows, widths and text labels;
- Two entry points: standalone canvases (created in the creation area) and flow canvases (embedded in body text);
- AI drawing: describe the change you want, the AI proposes an edit list first, and applies it after confirmation; drag-select a region to limit changes to the selection;
- Export as an image.

## Mobile

- Add text and shapes (rounded rectangles, ellipses, diamonds, triangles, arrows, notes);
- Drag to move, resize with the handles, connect objects;
- Background and object colors are adjustable, with undo and redo;
- Pinch to zoom, one finger to pan.

Complex desktop shapes — flowchart symbols, layers and so on — show read-only on mobile; editing happens back on the desktop.

images.body:
The image library keeps every image used in your writing in one place.

## Where images come from

- Local upload and paste;
- The AI image workbench — see [AI Image Generation](#/ai/imageGen);
- Right-click save from the built-in browser — see [Browser & Web Clipping](#/materials/browser);
- Phone camera or album inserts, filed automatically into `images/`.

## Organizing and filtering

- Grid and list views, with a favorites wall for the ones you reach for often;
- Filter by source (upload / AI generated / web saved), folder, tag and sort order;
- Immersive preview flips through images; the detail drawer shows the full-size image and its info;
- Multi-select for batch delete or download.

Writing illustrations and WeChat covers both draw from here.

wechat.body:
WeChat publishing comes from the official "微信公众号发布" plugin: typeset Markdown into an article, then drop it into the WeChat draft box.

Install and enable the plugin from the plugin center first (it requires 大懒猫写作 0.4.5 or later) — see [Official Plugins](#/plugins/official).

## Step 1: typeset

Click "发布当前文章" (publish this article) in an article to open the typeset-and-publish panel:

- Several layout themes set fonts, colours and quote styles in one go; code blocks, formulas, Mermaid charts and captions all render;
- Body images are compressed and uploaded automatically, and the cover can come from the asset library or be cropped on the spot;
- The typeset result can also be copied into the WeChat editor by hand.

No desktop app? The portal's online tools include a [WeChat typesetter](/tools/wechat/) that needs no sign-in.

## Step 2: configure the account

1. Sign in to the WeChat MP platform (mp.weixin.qq.com) and copy the **AppID** from "开发 → 基本设置"; click "生成" for the **AppSecret** and keep it safe — resetting it invalidates the old value immediately;
2. Open the plugin's "公众号账号设置" page, fill in AppID and AppSecret, then click "保存配置";
3. Click "检查微信 IP 白名单": if this machine's egress IP is not on the list, WeChat rejects the API call — add the IP under "开发 → 基本设置 → IP 白名单" and try again;
4. The AppSecret lives in the system credential store, never in plain text, and never syncs to another device.

## Step 3: publish the draft

1. Back in the article, click "发布当前文章";
2. Body images and the cover upload to the WeChat asset library and a draft is created;
3. Preview and mass-send still happen inside the WeChat MP platform.

## When the connection fails

The publishing API is WeChat's, so check in this order:

- **IP allowlist**: after an egress IP change (new network, router restart) re-add it, then click "检查微信 IP 白名单" in the plugin settings to confirm;
- **AppSecret**: check it was not reset or mistyped; "测试连接" verifies it;
- **API permissions**: the account must be WeChat-verified, otherwise the draft API is unavailable.

Only want the layout? Copy the typeset result — no account needed.

mobileShare.body:
Turn what you wrote into something shareable, straight from your phone.

## Four ways to share

Tap "Share" at the top right of the editor:

- **Long image**: rendered at 1440 px wide with the current theme — ready for social platforms;
- **Text**: plain text to any app;
- **PDF**: a formatted PDF file;
- **Markdown**: the `.md` source, to continue on the desktop.

All of them go through the system share sheet — send to WeChat, email, cloud drives or anything else. The footer carries the BigLazyCat Writer signature.

mcp.body:
MCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open protocol that lets AI clients drive software. The desktop app ships a built-in MCP service: once enabled, external AI such as Codex and Claude Code can create chapters, edit body text, and operate mind maps and canvases — 51 tools in total, all running locally.

## Enable the service

1. Open "Settings → MCP services" and switch it on — effective immediately, no restart;
2. The service listens only on the local loopback (random port); the token regenerates on every launch, so other devices cannot reach it.

## The 51 tools

The tool list in settings lets you untick anything you don't need — effective immediately — with "all on / read-only / all off" shortcuts. Nine groups:

- **Guidance & context** (8): read the usage guide and current context, capture creation views, locate resource files — how external AI gets to know your workspace;
- **Content & chapters** (16): list, read and create chapters and collections, write body text per revision, patch articles, full-text search, bulk import, tagging;
- **Creative artifacts** (4): manage mind maps and other artifact files;
- **Mind maps** (3): create from outline text, edit nodes incrementally, import `.xmind`;
- **Flow canvases** (4): create and update flow canvases;
- **Whiteboards** (3): read snapshots, batch add / edit / delete elements, connect, align, distribute, group;
- **Images** (5): list, read, import and update images;
- **Themes** (5): list, validate, install and delete themes — external AI can write and install themes for you, see [Themes & Appearance](#/writing/appearance);
- **Tasks & records** (3): check operation status, cancel running operations, view recent writes.

## Attach a client

1. Open "Settings → MCP services" and confirm the service is on;
2. Click "Detect" to find installed clients (Claude Code, Codex CLI, OpenCode, Gemini CLI), then "One-click write" to register BigLazyCat — the original config file is backed up first;
3. For other clients, copy the ready-made snippets under "Manual configuration".

How it works in one line: the client launches the app's bundled bridge with `--mcp-stdio`, and the bridge forwards requests to the running app. No app running, no tools.

After connecting, have the AI call `get_app_guide` first to learn the ropes. Step-by-step setup for Claude Desktop, Qoder, WorkBuddy and more: [MCP Client Setup](#/publish/mcpClients).

## Write safety

- Every write returns a receipt — which file changed, the version, the content hash — taken from the actual disk result; submitting the same operation twice writes once;
- Before an external edit lands, the previous chapter version is kept automatically and can be rolled back in [Version History](#/writing/versionHistory);
- Call logs keep 30 days and record identities only, never body text;
- Quitting from the system tray stops the service immediately; closing the window alone does not quit — the service stays available.

## Caveats

Cancelling a running operation does not roll back what was already submitted. After a mind map or canvas accepts external changes, its old undo history is cleared.

mcpClients.body:
With the MCP service on, register the app in your AI client's config and Codex, Claude, Qoder, WorkBuddy and friends can create chapters, edit body text and operate mind maps and canvases directly. Enabling the service and tool permissions: [MCP & External AI](#/publish/mcp). This page is the per-client setup.

## How it works

Every client launches the app's bundled bridge the same way: **command = the app's install path, args = `--mcp-stdio`**. While the app runs, the bridge forwards requests to it; without the app, tools are unavailable. Any client that supports "stdio MCP servers" can connect this way.

Don't hand-type the path: "Settings → MCP services → Manual configuration" generates ready-to-paste snippets pointing at your local install.

## Claude Code

**Automatic**: open "Settings → MCP services" and click "Detect" — on the Claude Code row click "One-click write"; it registers into `~/.claude.json` with a backup first.

**Manual** (pick one):

1. Command line: `claude mcp add biglazycat -- "<install path>" --mcp-stdio`;
2. Edit `~/.claude.json`, merging into `mcpServers`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "biglazycat": {
      "command": "<install path>",
      "args": ["--mcp-stdio"]
    }
  }
}
```

3. For a single project: put the same entry into `.mcp.json` in the project root.

Restart Claude Code for the config to take effect.

## Claude Desktop

Claude Desktop reads the same `mcpServers` shape from `claude_desktop_config.json` (Windows: `%APPDATA%\Claude\`, macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/`). Merge the `biglazycat` entry, restart, and the tools appear in the conversation's tool menu.

## Codex CLI

**Automatic**: after "Detect", click "One-click write" on the Codex CLI row; it registers into `~/.codex/config.toml`.

**Manual**: add to `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[mcp_servers.biglazycat]
command = '<install path>'
args = ["--mcp-stdio"]
```

Single-quoted TOML literals keep Windows backslashes as is. Restart Codex CLI.

## Qoder

1. Open settings in Qoder (`Ctrl+Shift+,`, macOS `⌘⇧,`) and choose "MCP" in the sidebar;
2. Click "+ Add" at the top right of "My services", transport **STDIO**;
3. Name `biglazycat`, command = the app's install path (copy from "Manual configuration"), args `--mcp-stdio`, save;
4. Or from the command line: `qoder mcp add biglazycat -- "<install path>" --mcp-stdio`.

Available in Qoder sessions right after saving.

## WorkBuddy

1. Open "Settings → Connectors → Add service", transport **stdio**, command = the app's install path, args `--mcp-stdio`;
2. Or edit `~/.workbuddy/mcp.json` directly (Windows: `.workbuddy\mcp.json` in your user folder), merging the same `mcpServers` entry as Claude;
3. **WorkBuddy requires manual trust for new servers**: find biglazycat under connector management and click "Trust".

## OpenCode and Gemini CLI

Both are on the "Detect" list — one click registers them. Manual snippets are under "Manual configuration" too: OpenCode uses a local server under the `mcp` key (command as an array), Gemini CLI uses `mcpServers` in `~/.gemini/settings.json`. Restart after configuring.

## Once connected

- Have the AI call `get_app_guide` first to learn your workspace before touching anything;
- Untick tools you don't want to expose under "Settings → MCP services" — effective immediately;
- Body-text writes go through the app's confirmation flow; changes roll back via [Version History](#/writing/versionHistory);
- Every call is logged under "Settings → AI call logs".

## Not working?

- The app must be running: quitting from the system tray stops the service — closing the window alone does not;
- Written configs take effect after restarting that client;
- Installed a new client? Click "Re-detect";
- If a config file with comments blocks the automatic write, merge manually as prompted;
- "A same-name config exists but was not written by this app" means a conflicting entry — check it manually before merging.

official.body:
The official plugins are maintained alongside the desktop app; their sources and installable packages are public on GitHub, and they double as reference implementations of the plugin system. Install from the desktop "Plugin center", or download the `.tgz` package from a repo's GitHub Releases and import it via "Plugin center → Install plugin" (sources are listed under "Install and enable" in [Plugin Development Process](#/plugins/dev)).

## Typeset export (blc-export-book)

Typesets articles and folders into print- and distribution-ready deliverables; Typst, fonts and other typesetting resources ship offline with the plugin — nothing is downloaded at runtime.

- Export a single article as PDF, Word (.docx), HTML, PNG or EPUB; make a book from a folder as EPUB, PDF, Word or HTML, with chapter selection and ordering in the typesetting workbench;
- Typesetting settings cover paper, font size, line spacing, margins, headers and footers, page numbers, cover and table of contents; settings and covers persist in the plugin's workspace storage;
- The image designer offers long images, poster ratios, four layouts and LXGW WenKai color themes;
- Embedded canvases and mind maps render from their original files, keeping layout, connectors, icons and formulas.

Entry: "排版导出" in the article editor's plugin menu and the workspace context menu. Usage: see [Article Typesetting Workbench](#/publish/typeset) and [Book Making & Export](#/publish/book). Repo: <https://github.com/biglazycat-writer/blc-export-book>

## WeChat publishing (blc-plugin-publish)

Typesets Markdown into WeChat-compatible rich articles and delivers them straight to the Official Account draft box.

- 10 typesetting themes with code highlighting, KaTeX formulas, Mermaid diagrams, image captions and a table of contents, exported as WeChat-compliant body HTML;
- Image handling: compressed inline image upload, permanent cover asset upload with crop parameters, and a library picker;
- Creates drafts through the official WeChat API: large articles are transferred in segments with SHA-256 integrity checks; the AppSecret lives in the system credential store, never in plain text;
- Pages follow the host's light/dark mode, with Chinese and English UI.

Entry: "发布当前文章" in the article plugin menu and toolbar; account setup lives in the plugin's settings page. Usage: see [WeChat Setup & Publishing](#/publish/wechat). Repo: <https://github.com/biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-publish>

## Source as documentation

Both plugins and the [plugin template](#/plugins/template) build on the same SDK. To see how a host capability is used in a real plugin — chunked document reads, large file saves, page state restore, document navigation — search for the command id in their sources.

template.body:
[blc-plugin-template](https://github.com/biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-template) is the official starting point for plugins: background commands, a browser page, state saving, build config, tests and a release workflow are all set up — clone it, rename a few things and start writing your own plugin instead of guessing manifest formats from an empty directory.

What's inside:

- Example background commands and a browser view (official SDK v1, pure TypeScript; the page uses plain DOM and can switch to React/Vue);
- Page state persistence and save-contract examples, plus `node:test` suites that mock the PluginContext and never need the host running;
- `npm run package` produces `dist-package/<name>-<version>.tgz` with a SHA-256 file in one step;
- A GitHub Actions release workflow that builds, tests, packages and creates a Release on `v*` tags; package name, version and artifact names are read from `package.json`, so it works unchanged after renaming.

## Starting from the template

The recommended route is the GitHub template: open the repo and click "Use this template", or run `gh repo create my-plugin --template biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-template --private --clone`. You can also clone it and point the remote at your own repository, download the ZIP, or call `plugin_project_create` from the app's MCP service (attach via [MCP & External AI](#/publish/mcp)).

## Must-change checklist

The template README has the full list; the essentials, followed by `npm run build && npm test`:

1. `name`, `description` and `version` in `package.json`;
2. `biglazycat.displayName` / `description` — how the plugin appears in the host;
3. The command id prefix (template default `template.*`): the manifest's `contributes.commands[].id` and `ctx.commands.register(...)` in `src/main.ts` must match verbatim, or the host refuses to enable the plugin;
4. View and menu ids and titles, plus the repo links in the README.

Manifest fields are constrained by the SDK's `manifest.schema.json`; field-by-field notes in [SDK Manifest Schema](#/plugins/sdkManifest), runtime setup and the full process in [Plugin Development Process](#/plugins/dev). The official [Typeset export and WeChat publishing](#/plugins/official) plugins are fuller reference implementations of the same structure.

dev.body:
Plugins are extensions that run inside the 大懒猫写作 desktop app: they can provide standalone pages, commands, menu entries, custom file editors, background scheduled tasks, and services for other plugins to call. The official ["WeChat publishing" and "Typeset export"](#/plugins/official) features are themselves plugins. A plugin runs in its own process with the current user's permissions and may use standard Node file, network and subprocess capabilities; process isolation guards against ordinary faults, it is not a security sandbox — only develop and install code you trust. For interface details see [Plugin SDK Overview](#/plugins/sdk).

## Prepare the runtime

The plugin backend runs on Node 22 (22.23.3 or newer, below 23; npm 10.9.9 or newer). The installer does not bundle Node/npm. Before developing, open "Settings → Runtime" and pick one:

- **Local install**: install Node yourself and click "Detect and use local runtime"; point to the Node executable manually if it is not on PATH;
- **App-managed version**: click "Download the managed version" — the app fetches the build for your system architecture from Node.js official servers, verifies SHA-256 and stores it in the app data directory without touching system settings; it works offline after download.

No restart is needed after configuration; before switching runtimes, stop plugin backgrounds and tasks.

## Create a project

Any of the three routes yields an ordinary npm project:

- **Template repo (recommended)**: open [biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-template](https://github.com/biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-template) and click "Use this template", or run `gh repo create my-plugin --template biglazycat-writer/blc-plugin-template --private --clone`. The template ships background commands, a browser page, state saving, packaging scripts and tests — rename a few things per its README checklist and start coding (details in [Plugin Template](#/plugins/template));
- **External coding tools**: enable the connection under System settings → MCP services and tick the "plugin development & management" tools so Codex, Claude Code etc. can attach via the app's `--mcp-stdio` (setup at [MCP & External AI](#/publish/mcp)), then call `plugin_project_create` to generate a standard TypeScript project in an empty directory; editing and version control stay in your own tools;
- **Manual**: create an npm project and depend on the public npm package `blc-plugin-sdk` — no vendored copy or global install needed.

The `biglazycat` field in `package.json` is the single plugin manifest:

```json
{
  "name": "my-plugin",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "type": "module",
  "engines": { "node": ">=22.23.3 <23.0.0" },
  "dependencies": { "blc-plugin-sdk": "^1.3.0" },
  "scripts": { "typecheck": "tsc --noEmit", "build": "tsc" },
  "biglazycat": {
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "displayName": "My plugin",
    "main": "dist/main.js",
    "platforms": ["windows/amd64", "darwin/arm64", "linux/amd64"],
    "contributes": {
      "commands": [{ "id": "hello", "title": "Run example", "aiCallable": true }]
    }
  }
}
```

Field-by-field notes live in [SDK Manifest Schema](#/plugins/sdkManifest); the authoritative signatures are `manifest.schema.json` and `index.d.ts` shipped with the SDK. The host delivers new capabilities by API revision (document export = revision 1, color scheme & user entries = revision 2, document navigation = revision 3); declare `minimumApiRevision` when you rely on one. Commit sources, build scripts, tests and `package-lock.json`; exclude `node_modules`.

## Implement the entry

The `main` backend entry exports a `definePlugin` call. Register every command and service during activation, matching the manifest — never register lazily on first call. The activate function may return a cleanup function that runs when the plugin is deactivated.

```ts
import { definePlugin } from 'blc-plugin-sdk'

export default definePlugin(ctx => {
  ctx.commands.register('hello', async (_args, invocation) => {
    invocation.signal.throwIfAborted()
    await invocation.progress(0.5, 'Working')
    return { message: 'Hello, 大懒猫 Writer!' }
  })
  return async () => {
    // release connections, listeners, etc.
  }
})
```

A page-only plugin may omit `main`; a background-only plugin needs no page. ESM/CJS, dynamic imports and standard Node APIs all work; native modules must match the target OS, architecture and Node ABI. A page `entry` points to a plain HTML file in the build output — build pages with Vite, React, Vue or any browser toolchain, and talk to the host with `connect()` from `blc-plugin-sdk/browser`; importing Node modules in the browser is not allowed.

## Typecheck and build

TypeScript must be compiled by real project scripts; running TS directly on Node (type stripping) does not count:

```bash
npm run typecheck   # tsc --noEmit
npm run build       # tsc (plus your page bundler)
```

"Typecheck" and "build" are separate steps reported separately; install preparation and development-mode auto-builds run exactly these two scripts.

## Install and enable

1. Open the main navigation "Plugin center" → "Install plugin";
2. Pick a source: a local project directory, a `.zip/.tgz` package, an npm name with an exact version, or a Git HTTPS URL plus a full 40-character commit SHA;
3. Packages that already ship JS and page artifacts install directly; source projects tick "prepare dependencies, typecheck and build", which requires `package-lock.json` and runs real npm scripts;
4. Review and accept the code-trust notice, then watch progress and build logs under "runs & install history";
5. Enable, open or stop the plugin from the list; expand "details" to see pages, commands, entry points and automatic events.

When developing with external AI tools everything can also run over MCP: `plugin_install` with a local directory plus `development:true`, `prepare:false`, `trusted:true` and a unique `requestId` returns a task; then `plugin_enable` and `plugin_open_view` to preview inside the app.

## Development mode

A local directory installed in development mode gets auto-refresh: the app watches the sources every second, and after two stable reads runs typecheck & build in an isolated directory, swaps in the successful artifacts and reopens the same page instance with its saved state; `dist`, `node_modules`, `.git`, `.cache` and `coverage` never trigger builds.

- When a build fails the last working page stays; inspect `plugin_diagnostics` and `plugin_task` (or the diagnostics panel in plugin details) for logs;
- Refresh is postponed while tasks are running or pages are unsaved;
- Install dependencies in the external project first — development mode never installs them for you; stopping the plugin stops the watcher, uninstalling keeps the sources;
- Credentials belong in the backend credentials store only — never in the manifest, sources or page state.

## Developing with external AI tools (optional)

After attaching a coding tool, a good sequence is:

1. Let the AI read `plugin_sdk` and `get_app_guide(section:"plugins")` first — manifest, SDK types, page bridge and save contract;
2. `plugin_project_create` to scaffold;
3. `plugin_project_run` for lock/install/typecheck/build/test in order, reading results with `plugin_task`;
4. `plugin_install` in development mode, then `plugin_enable`, then `plugin_open_view` to preview;
5. After edits, development mode rebuilds automatically; diagnose with `plugin_diagnostics` and `plugin_task`.

MCP write operations must carry a unique `requestId` — reuse it when retrying the same action, use a new one per action; installing and running scripts require `trusted:true`. Only commands marked `aiCallable:true` in the manifest are exposed to external AI, and external models cannot inject workspace identity.

## Package and distribute

The install center accepts four sources:

- **Plugin package**: `.zip` or `.tgz` containing build artifacts (JS, page HTML and dependencies); the template's `npm run package` produces `dist-package/<name>-<version>.tgz` plus a SHA-256 file in one step; native modules must cover every target platform;
- **npm package**: publish to the npm registry; installers enter the package name with an exact version;
- **Git**: HTTPS URL plus a full 40-character commit SHA; never put credentials or tokens in the URL — the plugin center rejects URLs with userinfo, query strings or fragments;
- **Local directory**: for internal tools and in-development distribution.

The manifest's `platforms` declares supported systems and architectures and the host enforces it. For the full interface, limits and error codes see [Plugin SDK Overview](#/plugins/sdk).

sdkPage.body:
API reference for plugin pages (browser side): the bridge, restore state, the save contract and document navigation.

## Pages, state & saving (browser side)

`const sdk = await connect()` returns a BrowserAPI:

| Member | Signature | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| context | context(): Promise<ViewSession> | Current view session (state, theme, color scheme) |
| setState | setState(state, dirty?) | Write the page's restore state — a JSON checkpoint up to 64 KiB; one in-flight request and the latest snapshot win, batched writes share results |
| onSave | onSave(handler): () => void | Register a save handler; returns an unsubscribe function |
| commands.invoke | invoke(command, args?): Promise<Task> | Invoke one of this plugin's commands, returns a host task |
| tasks.list | list(): Promise<summaries[]> | Summaries of this plugin's tasks; no result payload — hasResult marks availability so large results never flood control frames |
| tasks.get | get(id): Promise<Task> | Full state and result of one task; only this plugin's tasks |
| tasks.cancel | cancel(id): Promise<void> | Request cancellation — cooperative |
| appearance | getColorScheme() / onDidChangeColorScheme(listener) | Revision 2: read the host-resolved light / dark synchronously and subscribe to changes; the host sets `data-color-scheme` before the first paint |
| ready | ready(): Promise<void> | Revision 2: call after the first usable frame (including an error screen) so the host ends its loading hint; do not wait for requestAnimationFrame |

Save-contract example:

```ts
const view = await sdk.context()
let draft = view.state ?? { text: '' }

async function edited(text: string) {
  draft = { text }
  await sdk.setState(draft, true)
}

sdk.onSave(async () => {
  // commit to your own resource first; throw to stay dirty on failure
  await persistDraft(draft)
  await sdk.setState(draft, false)
})
await sdk.ready()
```

- The host keys state by pluginId/viewId/instanceId/workspaceId/resource so it never restores onto another file;
- Themes arrive as CSS variables in `view.theme` and update dynamically; the title, back and refresh controls are rendered by the plugin itself (revision 3) — the host only provides the container and failure recovery;
- Concurrency: at most 32 in-flight SDK requests per page, 16 concurrent operations per page in the host; exceeding returns `BACKPRESSURE`;
- On bridge timeout or lost connection show an error; never blindly replay side effects;
- Closing a page releases the bridge and renderer but keeps background tasks running; save confirmation waits for committed state, timeout reports `SAVE_TIMEOUT`.

### Document navigation (revision 3)

`sdk.ui.openDocument({workspaceId, workspaceSession, documentId})` locates and opens a document in the main window, reopening the tab if it was closed; collection documents resolve to their chapter. Navigation binds to the workspace session from page open, waits for save coordination and main-window confirmation; cancellation returns `NAVIGATION_CANCELLED`, timeout `NAVIGATION_TIMEOUT`, workspace changes `WORKSPACE_CHANGED`. Plugins decide themselves whether to save their draft before navigating: persist, wait for success, then navigate.
